cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "Please cite this repository with the metadata below."
title: "Measured steps toward a logic-based language model with Tsetlin Machines"
abstract: >-
  This repository holds the code, the registrations and the results pages of
  ten studies (19 Sep to 12 Nov 2026) that take measured steps toward a
  language model built from Tsetlin Machine clauses; run folders and
  datasets are not distributed. The first study built a token-level Tsetlin
  Machine language model on a sparse event-driven engine and compared it
  with a Kneser-Ney 5-gram model on the same tokens: its reproducibility
  hypotheses were met at the scale tested, and its main accuracy goal was
  missed on the one seed that was run, which triggered the registered kill
  criterion. The second study tested the hierarchical Tsetlin Machine
  procedure of an unpublished project proposal on artificial data, two
  sentence families, news topics and Wikipedia paragraph pairs and ended
  mostly negative: its single final test is below word overlap on both
  Wikipedia tasks. The third study is preparation for a word-level clause
  language model, and the registered test of the router it reproduced is
  negative. The fourth study tested the Tsetlin Machine as the decision
  layer over language encoders, from intent routing to agents that choose
  the next action in dialogues: with the same tuning, an agent with no
  neural network at any stage is not worse, within the registered margin,
  than an agent that runs a transformer encoder per decision on three
  dialogue test splits, the registered test of superiority is not met, and
  the hierarchical machine of the proposal's package beats the flat machine
  of the same package on one workflow task. The fifth study holds the first
  steps of a count-based language model: in its test T0 the learned votes
  of a Tsetlin Machine beat a counted read-out, and in A2 an exchange-class
  code lowers the perplexity of a count model. Four studies added on 29 Oct
  2026 ship small result files. In one of them the same agent is not worse
  than a prompted local 35B LLM by 3 points on three test sets (on one of
  them on seed 1 only) and decides in milliseconds on one CPU thread
  against seconds on a GPU, while TF-IDF logistic regression is level with
  it and a fine-tuned transformer is ahead; static word tables, guards
  around an LLM agent and a Tsetlin Machine that writes text gave mixed or
  negative results. The tenth study tests a learning rule for OR-groups of
  words on tasks of the second study.
authors:
